The decisive triage point is already clear: a general \(k\)-factor is algorithmically obtainable, so it cannot support an honest Track A claim. The paper’s bounded-degree spanning-tree problem is NP-complete, but the paper gives only worst-case hardness—not distributional hardness. I’m therefore evaluating a Track B factor family, where the polynomial reference algorithm is disclosed and the benchmark tests whether the solver notices a short graph symmetry instead of simulating that algorithm.
exec
/bin/bash -lc "rg -l 'TRACK = \"B\"' ../*/gen_*.py | head -20
sed -n '1,260p' ../../scripts/harden.py
sed -n '1,260p' ../../scripts/submit.sh" in /home/gson/gvrepo/results/2308.13874
