Completed a verified Track B problem generator based on the paper’s native 1-factor objects.

- [Generator](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2308.13874/gen_2308_13874.py)
- [README](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2308.13874/README.md)
- [Self-test report](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2308.13874/selftest_report.json)
- [Bare oracle transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2308.13874/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [Structural-hint transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2308.13874/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [Placebo-hint transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2308.13874/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

Results:

- All G1–G9(c) gates pass.
- Shipping preset: `hard`, `n=251`, `d=17`.
- Bare oracle: easy 1/3 solved, medium 1/3, hard 0/3—verdict `hardened`.
- G9: structural 3/3, placebo 1/3; structural lift `0.667`.
- Answer: 895 characters, 251 atoms.
- Compact route: 297 operations.
- Reference Hopcroft–Karp: 10,765 counted operations, under one millisecond.
- Generation is pure inverse generation and runs no solver or attack.
- The README documents that the current repository harness used its configured two-vendor pool, despite older task text describing four vendors.

The family follows the paper’s Section 1 definition of a 1-factor, confirmed against the [full arXiv paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2308.13874v1).
